    "text": "To determine the length of the belt, we need to consider the configuration of the three circles and the belt that wraps around them. The belt forms a path that consists of three arcs and three straight segments.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the straight segments. The centers of the three circles form an equilateral triangle with each side equal to the diameter of the circles, which is \\(2 \\times 10 = 20\\) cm. Therefore, the length of each straight segment is 20 cm. Since there are three such segments, the total length of the straight segments is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 20 = 60 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the length of the three arcs. The belt forms three arcs that together make a full circle. The radius of the circle formed by the belt is the distance from the center of one circle to the point where the belt leaves that circle, which is the radius of the circle plus the radius of the circle, i.e., \\(10 + 10 = 20\\) cm. Therefore, the belt forms a circle with a radius of 20 cm, and the length of the belt is the circumference of this circle. The circumference of a circle is given by \\(2\\pi \\times \\text{radius}\\), so the length of the belt is:\n\\[\n2 \\times 20 \\pi = 40\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nAdding the lengths of the straight segments and the arcs, we get the total length of the belt:\n\\[\n60 + 40\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nIn the form \\(a + b\\pi\\), we have \\(a = 60\\) and \\(b = 40\\). Therefore, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[\n60 + 40 = 100\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{100}\n\\]",
    "timing_scope": "native prompt render, tokenize, transfer, CUDA-synchronized generate, decode; excludes loading/scoring",
